   315	the prompt's "find `r_c`".
   316	
   317	### 6.3 Where the ladder would have to reach
   318	
   319	`H^{c−1} ∣ P^c` gives `s ≥ ⌈(c−1)(K−1)/c⌉` and `t ≥ 1`, so
   320	`deg_y P ≥ ⌈(c−1)(K−1)/c⌉ + 1`. Against `deg_y P ≤ deg_y β ≤ K−1` this is a
   321	contradiction iff `c ≥ K`:
   322	
   323	```text
   324	   c = K  :  s >= ceil((K-1)^2/K) = K-1,  t >= 1  =>  deg_y P >= K  >  K-1   DEAD
   325	```
   326	
   327	Machine table `MARK C5` (`struct_checks.out`) prints the whole ladder for
   328	`K = 7, 8, 9, 12`; the first `DEAD` row is `c = K` in every case. So the ladder
   329	would close every row for every `K` — but its required depth is `K`, **not**
   330	bounded, and only `c = 2, 3` are proved.
    90	Write `(x, y) = (γ, π)`. Let `H := y^{K−1}(y−x)`, the two-point leading form:
    91	one root of multiplicity `V₂' = K−1` (the centre, at `y = 0`) and one simple
    92	root (at `y = x`). Normalising the centre to `y = 0` costs `y ↦ y + λx`
    93	(unimodular, fixes `x`, fixes `J`), and moving the second root to `y = x` costs
    94	`x ↦ μx` (rescales `c` only). The mirror normalisation `y(y−x)^{K−1}` is carried
    95	onto this one by `(x, y) ↦ (−x, y−x)`, which has determinant `−1` and fixes
    96	`x⁴`; so no generality is lost by the choice.
    97	
    98	### 2.2 The tower
    99	
   100	`h` := the **2nd approximate root of `f`**: the unique polynomial monic in `y`
   101	of `y`-degree `K` with `deg_y(f − h²) < K`. Put
   102	
   103	```
   104	f = h² + B ,        B := f − h² ,        deg_y B < K            (definition of h)
   105	g = h³ + a₂h² + a₁h + a₀ ,               deg_y a_i < K          (h-adic expansion; always valid)
   106	```
   107	
   108	The second line is division with remainder by the monic `h`, so it is
   109	unconditional. Setting `β := B/2` and `α := quo_y(β², h)`, the **tower** is
   110	
   111	```
   112	f = h² + 2β ,     g = h³ + 3βh + (3/2)α ,   i.e.   a₂ = 0, a₁ = 3β, a₀ = (3/2)α .
   113	```
   114	
   115	Derivation (not assumption): with `β² = αh + ρ`, `deg_y ρ < K`,
   116	
   117	```
   118	g² − f³ = (2ε − 3α)h³ − 3ρh² + 9αβh + (9/4)α² − 8β³        for  g = h³ + 3βh + ε,
   119	```
   120	
   121	so `ε = (3/2)α` is precisely the choice that annihilates the `h³` band —
   122	i.e. `g` is `f^{3/2}` truncated. Verified exactly:
   123	`box/k4ray-20260903/identity_check.out` `MARK_ID3_GSQ 0`.
   124	
   125	### 2.3 What is proved about the shape, and what is not
   126	
   127	* **`lf(f) = H²`, `lf(g) = H³`.** For `K ≥ 2` the top Jacobian band
   128	  `J(lf f, lf g)` sits in degree `5K−2 > 4` and must vanish; for binary forms
   129	  `J(F,G) = 0` with `deg F = 2K`, `deg G = 3K` forces `F³ = λG²`, hence
   130	  `F = H²`, `G = H³` for a common form `H` of degree `K`. Consequently
   131	  `deg B ≤ 2K−1`, `deg a₂ ≤ K−1`, `deg a₁ ≤ 2K−1`, `deg a₀ ≤ 3K−1`.
   132	* **`deg_y B < K` is PROVED**, being the defining property of the approximate
   133	  root. This discharges the prompt's step-(3) obligation *in the `y` direction*.
   134	* **`a₂ = 0`, `a₁ = 3β`, `a₀ = (3/2)α` is a normalisation with a residual
   135	  obligation.** Its top band is proved here: matching band `3K−1` gives
   136	  `g_{3K−1} = (3/2)H·f_{2K−1}`, and since the three `h`-adic pieces
   137	  `a₂H², Ha₁, a₀` occupy the *disjoint* `y`-degree ranges `[2K, 3K−1]`,
   138	  `[K, 2K−1]`, `[0, K−1]`, the band separates into
   139	  `(a₂)_{K−1} = 0`, `(a₀)_{3K−1} = 0`, `(a₁)_{2K−1} = (3/2)B_{2K−1}`.
   140	  The same `y`-degree separation is available at every band, so the induction is
   141	  routine; I did not write it out band by band and do **not** claim it as proved.
   142	  Everything in §3 is stated in the *general* shape (no tower) precisely so that
   143	  the composite arm does not depend on it.
   144	* **`deg β ≤ K−1` (total degree) is NOT proved.** Only `deg β ≤ 2K−1` is. This
   145	  is the single scoping restriction on §5 and is raised as
   146	  `OPEN[K4RAY-BETA-DEG]`.
   147	
   148	---
   149	
   150	## 3. The `B₂` dichotomy, arm (2): PROVED uniformly in `K`
   151	
   152	### 3.1 The general-shape identity
   153	
   154	For **any** `h, A₂, A₃, B₂` (no tower, no chart, no order basis), writing
   155	`J(u,v) := u_xv_y − u_yv_x`:
   156	
   157	> **IDENTITY (ID1).**
   158	> `J(h² + B₂ , h³ + A₂h + A₃) = h²[2J(h,A₂) − 3J(h,B₂)] + h[2J(h,A₃) + J(B₂,A₂)]
   159	>  − A₂J(h,B₂) + J(B₂,A₃)`
   160	
   161	Verified: `identity_check.out` `MARK_ID1_GENERAL_SHAPE 0`.
   162	
   163	### 3.2 The kill
   164	
   165	> **LEMMA B2-CONST (uniform in `K`, unconditional).** Let `f = h² + B₂`,
   166	> `g = h³ + A₂h + A₃` with `deg_y h = K ≥ 1`, `h` monic in `y`. If `B₂ ∈ k`
   167	> then
   168	> ```
   169	> J(f, g) = 2h·[ h·J(h,A₂) + J(h,A₃) ] ,      so   h ∣ J(f,g).
   170	> ```
   171	> Hence `J(f,g)` is **not** of the form `c·x⁴` with `c ≠ 0`: `h` is monic in `y`
   172	> of `y`-degree `K ≥ 1`, and no such polynomial divides `x⁴`. The same argument
   173	> kills `J = c·x^k` for every `k ≥ 0`, in particular the Keller condition `J = c`.
   174	
   175	Proof: `B₂ ∈ k` makes `J(h,B₂) = J(B₂,A₂) = J(B₂,A₃) = 0` in ID1. Verified:
   176	`MARK_ID4_HDIVJ 0` (the reduction of `J` modulo `(h)` is `0`) and
   177	`MARK_ID4_QUOT 0` (the displayed quotient identity). ∎
   178	
   179	### 3.3 On the tower it is the composite locus, and `J ≡ 0`
   180	
   181	If additionally `(A₂, A₃) = (3β, (3/2)α)` with `β = B₂/2 ∈ k`, then
   182	`β²` is a constant, `deg_y β² = 0 < K`, hence `α = quo_y(β², h) = 0` and
   183	
   184	```
   185	f = h² + 2β ,   g = h³ + 3βh   →   f = φ(h),  g = ψ(h)  with φ(T) = T² + 2β,  ψ(T) = T³ + 3βT,
   186	```
   187	
   188	so the pair is **composite** — `J(φ(h), ψ(h)) = φ'ψ'·J(h,h) = 0` identically —
   189	and a composite pair is not a Keller counterexample. Verified:
   190	`MARK_ID5_ALPHA_ZERO 0`, `MARK_ID5_J 0`.
   191	
   192	The kill is *not* the weaker condition `α = 0`: the control `β = x² − y` has
   193	`α = 0` but `J ≠ 0`. It needs `β` constant, nothing less. This reproduces, and
   194	now proves uniformly in `K`, the `K = 9` statement banked as
   195	PROVED-HERE[BRANCH-A-JACOBIAN-IDENTITY].
   196	
   197	---
   198	
   199	## 4. The nonconstant arm: a sharpened identity and a uniform TOP-BAND LEMMA
   200	
   201	### 4.1 Two sharpenings of the tower identity
   202	
   203	> **IDENTITY (ID2).** `J(f,g) = 3h·J(h,α) − 6β·J(h,β) + 3J(β,α)`.
   204	> **IDENTITY (ID6, sharper — new).** With `ρ := β² − αh` (so `deg_y ρ < K`),
   205	> ```
   206	>                J(f, g)  =  3·[ J(β, α) − J(h, ρ) ] .
   207	> ```
   208	
   209	ID6 follows from ID2 in one line: `2βJ(h,β) = J(h,β²) = hJ(h,α) + J(h,ρ)`, so
   210	the `3hJ(h,α)` term cancels exactly. Verified at `K = 4, 5, 7`:
   211	`identity2_check.out` `MARK_ID6_a/b/c` all `0`, with the attained `y`-degree
   212	bounds `deg_y J(h,ρ) = 2K−2`, `deg_y J(β,α) = 2K−4` (`MARK_ID6_degy_bounds`
   213	prints `12 12 10 10` at `K = 7`).
   214	
   215	ID6 is the right object: the whole Jacobian is a difference of two Wronskians,
   216	and the entire `α`-tower has been absorbed into the single remainder `ρ`.
   217	
   218	### 4.2 The top band
   219	
   220	Let `b := deg β ≥ 1` (total degree; `b ≤ 2K−1` by §2.3), `β_b` its leading form,
   221	and `R₀ := rem_y(β_b², H)`, a form of degree `2b` with `deg_y R₀ ≤ K−1`.
   222	Division of a form by the `y`-monic form `H` is degree-homogeneous, so `R₀` is
   223	exactly the degree-`2b` part of `ρ`.
   224	
   225	> **TOP-BAND IDENTITY.** `[J(f,g)]_{2b+K−2} = −3·J(H, R₀)`.
   226	
   227	Verified at `(K,b) = (5,6)` and `(4,5)`, and in an `R₀ = 0` instance where the
   228	predicted band collapse occurs (`deg J = 9 < 11 = 2b+K−2`):
   229	`topband_check.out` `MARK_TB1_resid 0`, `MARK_TB2_resid 0`, `MARK_TB3_R0 0`.
   230	
   231	> **TOP-BAND LEMMA (uniform in `K`).** For `K ≥ 4` and `β ∉ k`,
   232	> ```
   233	>            H ∣ β_b²   in k[x,y],  equivalently   y^{⌈(K−1)/2⌉}·(y−x)  ∣  β_b .
   234	> ```
   235	> In particular `b ≥ ⌈(K−1)/2⌉ + 1`, so `2b ≥ K+1`.
   236	
   237	*Proof.* Since `b ≤ 2K−1 < 2K`, the term `3J(β,α)` of ID6 has degree
   238	`≤ 3b−K−2 < 2b+K−2`, so the top band is the displayed one. If
   239	`2b+K−2 > 4` the band must vanish, i.e. `J(H, R₀) = 0`. Euler's relation gives
   240	`x·J(H,R) = K·H·R_y − 2b·H_y·R`, so `J(H,R₀) = 0` forces either `R₀ = 0` or
   241	`R₀^K = λH^{2b}` with `λ ≠ 0`. In the second case `H = y^{K−1}(y−x)` is not a
   242	proper power (the exponent gcd is `gcd(K−1,1) = 1`), so `R₀ = λ'H^{2b/K}` with
   243	`K ∣ 2b`, whence `deg_y R₀ = 2b`; but `deg_y R₀ ≤ K−1`, so `2b ≤ K−1 < K`, and
   244	with `K ∣ 2b` this forces `b = 0`, contradicting `β ∉ k`. Hence `R₀ = 0`, i.e.
   245	`y^{K−1}(y−x) ∣ β_b²`, i.e. `y^{⌈(K−1)/2⌉}(y−x) ∣ β_b`.
   246	
   247	The single excluded case is `2b+K−2 ≤ 4` with `b ≥ 1`, i.e. `(K,b) = (4,1)`.
   248	There `[J]_4 = −3J(H, β_1²) = −6β_1J(H,β_1)` must equal `c·x⁴`, so `β_1 ∣ x⁴`
   249	and `β_1 = μx`, `μ ≠ 0`; then `J(H, μx) = −μ(4y³ − 3xy²)` and
   250	`−6β_1J(H,β_1) = 6μ²x(4y³ − 3xy²) ≠ c·x⁴`. So `(K,b) = (4,1)` is dead outright. ∎
   251	
   252	This is a genuinely new, `K`-uniform constraint on the nonconstant arm: the
   253	leading form of `β` must already carry `⌈(K−1)/2⌉` powers of the centre
   254	direction **and** the second point. It is the structural half of what the
   255	"`B₂` lies in a bounded space" step wants, and it holds with no chart at all.
   256	
   257	---
   258	
   259	## 5. The bounded chart, exact over Q
   260	

