    84	
    85	## 3. Guided GB helper
    86	
    87	`box/lib/guided_gb.py` exposes:
    88	
    89	```python
    90	SingularSystem
    91	HilbertHint
    92	PromotionPolicy
    93	RunConfig
    94	guided_groebner
    95	prelude_from_script
    96	crt_pair
    97	crt_many
    98	rational_reconstruct
    99	```
   100	
   101	The helper emits a standalone Singular script from a caller-supplied prelude
   102	and generator list.  The prelude declares the ring and generator polynomials;
   103	the helper appends its own ideal, `std` call, and control battery.
   104	
   105	When a Hilbert hint is supplied, the emitted Singular call has the required
   106	Card A shape:
   107	
   108	```singular
   109	ideal GG_G_main=std(GG_I_main,GG_HNUM_main,GG_WTS_main);
   110	```
   111	
   112	The helper always runs Singular through `stdbuf`:
   113	
   114	```text
   115	stdbuf -oL -eL Singular --cpus=<n> --threads=<n> --flint-threads=<n> --no-rc -q <script>
   116	```
   117	
   118	The certificate for both T1 and T2 records this exact command prefix.  Stdout
   119	and stderr are streamed into files while the process runs, so marker output is
   120	not held only in a block-buffered parent process.
   121	
   122	Typed verdicts are:
   123	
   124	```text
   125	DIM0_CHAR0
   126	UNIT_IDEAL_CHAR0
   127	POSDIM
   128	MODULAR_ONLY
   129	INCONCLUSIVE_TIMEOUT
   130	```
   131	
   132	The mandatory controls in every accepted helper result are:
   133	
   134	* `NF(generator_i,G)=0` for every submitted generator;
   135	* `reduce(1,G)=0` recorded as the unit-ideal test;
   136	* `dim(G)` and basis size recorded;
   137	* `lead(G)`, `minbase(lead(G))`, and `std(minbase(lead(G)))` computed;
   138	* `vdim(std(minbase(lead(G))))` compared with the predicted length when a
   139	  predicted length is supplied;
   140	* a perturbed Hilbert-series negative control run rejected when
   141	  `RunConfig.run_perturbed_control=True`.
   142	
   143	The perturbed control deliberately changes the Hilbert numerator and, when a
   144	predicted length exists, the acceptance target.  This is stricter than simply
   145	asking whether Singular can still recover the true basis from a poor hint: a
   146	bad hint must not pass the bad certificate.
   147	
   148	Parallel good-prime support is by passing multiple `SingularSystem` objects to
   149	`guided_groebner`.  The runner uses a `ThreadPoolExecutor`, divides the
   150	configured total core budget over active jobs, and stores per-prime/fibre
   151	scripts and logs separately.  The certificate also records CRT plus bounded
   152	rational-reconstruction data for small modular scalar invariants when at least
   153	two modular runs are present.  The exported CRT functions are available for
   154	future lanes that want to lift named modular scalar residues explicitly.
   155	
   156	## 4. Promotion scope
   157	
   158	The helper encodes FALLACY-v2's modular-promotion guard as an explicit
   159	`PromotionPolicy`.  Modular evidence is not silently promoted.
   160	
   161	The only modular promotion enabled by `PromotionPolicy.homogeneous_properness()`
   162	is:
   163	
   164	```text
   165	homogeneous ideal + positive weights + accepted modular dim=0
   166	  => DIM0_CHAR0
   167	```
   168	
   169	This is the properness scope from the charged `k16-properness` audit: for an
   170	ideal generated by positive-weight homogeneous polynomials over the local base,
   171	an empty projective special fibre, equivalently affine cone `{0}`, promotes to
   172	the characteristic-zero dimension-zero cone conclusion.
   173	
   174	Explicit non-promotions:
   175	
   176	```text
   177	modular dim > 0  => no characteristic-zero conclusion
   178	modular unit     => no characteristic-zero unit conclusion by default
   179	inhomogeneous modular unit => MODULAR_ONLY unless exact-Q evidence is supplied
   180	```
   181	
   182	Exact-Q computations may return `UNIT_IDEAL_CHAR0` directly when
   183	`reduce(1,G)==0`.  T2 uses this exact-Q route for the D=108 stage-0 common-h3
   184	unit replay.
   185	
   186	## 5. Staged band emitter
   187	
   188	`box/lib/staged_band_emitter.py` exposes:
   189	
   190	```python
   191	BandRow
   192	BandEmitConfig
   193	read_order_rows_tsv
   194	emit_staged_band_files
   195	emit_order_chart_bands
   196	generator_checksum
   197	```
   198	
   199	The emitter consumes the `order_basis_full.py` row TSV format:
   200	
   201	```text
   202	source_index|h_power|x_power|y_power|expr
   203	```
   204	
   205	Rows are assigned to a staged band and weighted degree.  For the order-basis
   206	regression, the grouping is `(h_power, x_power + y_power)`.  Each group is
   207	written as a separate `.sing` file with the same ring declaration and only that
   208	band's generators.  Shared localization generators, such as `T*(c)-1`, are
   209	written once to `global_generators.sing`.
   210	
   211	The manifest records:
   212	
   213	```text
   214	band file paths and SHA-256 hashes
   215	per-band row counts and row labels
   216	global-generator file hash
   217	generator_union_checksum
   218	monolithic_generator_checksum
   219	union_equals_monolithic
   220	```
   221	
   222	The checksum is order-independent over labelled generator text.  This lets a
   223	later solver consume completed band files incrementally and still verify that
   224	the union is the same generator set as the monolithic ideal would have used.
   225	
