    90	Write `(x, y) = (γ, π)`. Let `H := y^{K−1}(y−x)`, the two-point leading form:
    91	one root of multiplicity `V₂' = K−1` (the centre, at `y = 0`) and one simple
    92	root (at `y = x`). Normalising the centre to `y = 0` costs `y ↦ y + λx`
    93	(unimodular, fixes `x`, fixes `J`), and moving the second root to `y = x` costs
    94	`x ↦ μx` (rescales `c` only). The mirror normalisation `y(y−x)^{K−1}` is carried
    95	onto this one by `(x, y) ↦ (−x, y−x)`, which has determinant `−1` and fixes
    96	`x⁴`; so no generality is lost by the choice.
    97	
    98	### 2.2 The tower
    99	
   100	`h` := the **2nd approximate root of `f`**: the unique polynomial monic in `y`
   101	of `y`-degree `K` with `deg_y(f − h²) < K`. Put
   102	
   103	```
   104	f = h² + B ,        B := f − h² ,        deg_y B < K            (definition of h)
   105	g = h³ + a₂h² + a₁h + a₀ ,               deg_y a_i < K          (h-adic expansion; always valid)
   106	```
   107	
   108	The second line is division with remainder by the monic `h`, so it is
   109	unconditional. Setting `β := B/2` and `α := quo_y(β², h)`, the **tower** is
   110	
   111	```
   112	f = h² + 2β ,     g = h³ + 3βh + (3/2)α ,   i.e.   a₂ = 0, a₁ = 3β, a₀ = (3/2)α .
   113	```
   114	
   115	Derivation (not assumption): with `β² = αh + ρ`, `deg_y ρ < K`,
   116	
   117	```
   118	g² − f³ = (2ε − 3α)h³ − 3ρh² + 9αβh + (9/4)α² − 8β³        for  g = h³ + 3βh + ε,
   119	```
   120	
   121	so `ε = (3/2)α` is precisely the choice that annihilates the `h³` band —
   122	i.e. `g` is `f^{3/2}` truncated. Verified exactly:
   123	`box/k4ray-20260903/identity_check.out` `MARK_ID3_GSQ 0`.
